From e92f202660417284267732958d6055d9c7f9eeed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JorreFog <JorreFog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 18:04:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NDS: MELONDS_IOSTATS counts the slow memory accesses;
 MELONDS_IOFAST direct routes for the hot ones

The JIT sends every access fast memory can't take (IO, VRAM, LDM/STM, pages holding compiled code) to
SlowRead9/SlowWrite9/SlowBlockTransfer9 (and the ARM7 ones), which go through NDS::ARM9Read*/ARM9Write*
(virtual), a switch on the region, NDS::ARM9IORead*/ARM9IOWrite* (virtual), a switch on the register,
and for the 3D engine GPU3D::Write32, per word.

MELONDS_IOSTATS=n (from frame n on; 1 = all) counts, and prints the top entries to stderr at exit:
- the JIT's slow reads and writes per CPU, by address for IO and by region otherwise (ITCM/DTCM apart),
- its block transfers per CPU, by the first word's address, in calls and words,
- every IO register access by address, whoever makes it (JIT, interpreter, DMA), and the JIT's calls to
  GPU3D for a known address.
Off, each counting site is one branch on a global that is never taken. NDS_IOFast.cpp holds it.

MELONDS_IOFAST=0 turns off the direct routes the slow paths now take on the DS (not the DSi, which
overrides those functions), each doing exactly what the chain does for that address, minus the
dispatch:
- main RAM: reads, and writes with the JIT's CheckAndInvalidate first (pages holding code fault into
  the slow path; every LDM is a slow block transfer),
- VRAM: 16- and 32-bit reads and writes, with the same SyncVRAM_*/ReadVRAM_*/WriteVRAM_* calls,
- the GX FIFO (0x04000400-43F): GeometryEnabled, then WriteToGXFIFO; the command ports
  (0x04000440-5FF): GPU3D::Write32, as ARM9IOWrite32 does,
- 32-bit reads of DISPSTAT+VCOUNT, IME, IE, IF; 16-bit reads of DISPSTAT, VCOUNT, KEYINPUT
  (clearing LagFrameFlag as ARM9IORead16 does), IME, IE and IF halves: plain reads of state.
SlowBlockTransfer9 does the TCM checks and the routes per word, without a call per word. The JIT
charges a slow access's cycles in the compiled code, from the decoder's DataCycles, so the routes
change no timing. The default is on. MELONDS_IOFAST_ROUTES=mask keeps only some of them, for A/B runs
of one route at a time: 1 main RAM, 2 VRAM, 4 the GX ports, 8 the IO register reads (default 0xF).
ARMJIT::Reset re-reads both variables, so a front-end can set them per console.
